Sorry,
It has been my experience that when I am "asked" to do something
by an Ed. ADmin type there is always an implicit "or else"
attached to the request. I assumed that Betty was useing the word
"asked" in this euphamistic(sp) way. If she really did just ask, I
have no problem with this.
I would, however, quibble with Wes' comparison of what I might put
in a finger file with a pin-up posed on the OUTSIDE of my door. I
think a better comparison might be to the kind of message I might
leave on my personal telephone answering machine. I really think
that if I want to greet people who dial my number with "hello, I'm
polymorphically perverse and show only enough respect for community
standards to stay out of jail. If you what to talk to me in spite
of/because of this, please leave your name and number and I'll get
back to you", the phone campany would have no business telling me
to change that message though they could certainly ask me to do so.
Even granting that the account on our University mainframe that I
enjoy as a student is a priviledge and not a right, to revoke that
priviledge in response to how I choose to self-describe in a finger
would be content based censorship in philosphical terms. I'm not
arguing legality here. I'm speaking to the personal values of the
people who have effective control of student access to university
accounts. Every Ed. Admin type I've ever dealt with has payed lip
service to freedom of expression, but I've never known a single one
to tolerate any actual exercise of this freedom in a situation where
the excercise was considered offensive and the ability to supress it
existed. On this basis, I assume that the support routinely voiced
by people like Betty for "freedom of speech" means in practice that
I can say anything I want as long as I don't say anything which the
Ed. Admin type feels a real need to prevent from being said. I may
be wrong in some cases and Bettys' may be one of them. But if she
is, she is a deviant within her occupational culture and the student
s at her institution are very, very lucky to have her.
                      Thanks,
