I am a grad student.  Last semester, I had a "Faculty Lecturer"
position teaching 50 engineering freshmen how to write programs.
I have also taught recitations (small classes) at another university.

I found this piece of "research" to be highly-insulting.  It is an
unjustified attack on graduate teaching assistants.  The author merely
juxtaposes two facts (declining standards and greater use of graduate
student help) and expects the reader to draw the conclusion that there
is some kind of causal connection between the two.  This is a favorite
media propaganda tactic (e.g., "During the Reagan years, AIDS cases
increased by 1000%"), and it's disappointing to see AIA stooping to a
tactic so often used by the PC.

>      The teaching assistants sometimes lecture to large classes,
> they often lead smaller discussion sections where the most
> crucial teaching should take place,

What evidence is there for this?  I have never seen students lecturing
to large classes.  They sometimes teach recitations, particularly in the
large freshmen classes, where sheer numbers make it difficult to provide
professors for every class.

>					they make up and grade
> examinations, they give final course  grades that become part of
> the undergraduates' official academic record--they even counsel
> students in both academic and career matters.

This is done under the supervision of the professor.  The professor
is like a manager who delegates subtasks to subordinates, reserving
for himself only those tasks requiring his special expertise, and
coordinating the actions of his team.  This technique has been used
for centuries (e.g., the apprenticeship systems	used in the past),
and does not indicate that the expert at the top of the hierarchy
doesn't care about the customer.  If one goes to a hospital for a
complex surgical procedure, does he whine about declining standards
if he discovers that the head surgeon let a junior surgeon sew up
the incisions?

>      The so-called teaching assistants are usually graduate
> students barely a step or two ahead of the students in their
> charge; some of them are even undergraduates.  As a group, they
> are manifestly unqualified to tackle the enormous responsibility
> the university entrusts to them.  Not a one of them could land a
> job on the faculty of a comparable university.  Why?  Simply
> because they have not earned their degree; they have not yet
> demonstrated enough mastery of a subject to be qualified for the
> Ph.D.

Ah, now we get to the main point: grad students (I don't include
undergrads, since I have never heard of one doing more than grade
assignments) are "manifestly unqualified" to teach.  Why are they
"manifestly unqualified"?  Because they don't have their Ph.D.'s!

This is an equivocation between two meanings of the word "qualified."
According to my online dictionary, two of the meanings of this word are:

	1a: fitted (as by training or experience) for a given purpose: COMPETENT
	1b: having complied with the specific requirements or precedent conditions
	     (as for an office or employment): ELIGIBLE

Mr. Anderson assumes that because a grad student is "unqualified" by
definition 1b (i.e., he does not yet have the credentials necessary to
become a faculty member), it must follow that he is "unqualified" by
definition 1a (i.e., he is incapable of teaching).  If this were true,
what would it take to make that student competent?  Getting the Ph.D.?
Perhaps Mr. Anderson's view of reality comes from the end of _The_Wizard_
_of_Oz_, where the Scarecrow instantly gains knowledge upon being handed
a diploma.  Unfortunately, the real world is more complex, and such
things as competence cannot be quantized into discrete events such as
the act of receiving a Ph.D.

Mr Anderson seems to assume that new professors are qualified to teach.
What is a professor qualified to teach?  Presumably, he has some breadth
in his field, plus depth in his specialty.  This would make him qualified
to teach most basic-level courses in the area, plus advanced courses in
his specialty.  (This leaves aside the issue of whether he has basic
teaching skills, or whether he has the proper chromosones or adequate
racial purity to fit current PC definitions of "qualified.")

Graduate schools typically require students to demonstrate breadth of
knowledge covering different areas within their major field.  This is
especially true for Ph.D. students.  For the typical Ph.D. student, the
only thing remaining between him and his degree is an extended study of
his specialty, culminating in a dissertation.  The general knowledge
he possesses is not likely to increase during this time.

Therefore, it follows that grad students possess sufficient knowledge
to teach low-level undergraduate courses.  They may even be qualified
to teach more advanced courses in their area of research, although
the scope of these courses would almost certainly be less than those
taught by an older professor.  Insulting language ("fake professors"),
alarmist polemics, and mislaid blame are not justified by the facts.

> [Statistics about how many TA's are in which school, etc.]

>      The extent of this spurious teaching is so great as to call
> into question the legitimacy of the degrees given by those
> universities that use students for professors.
>      Many Ivy League universities are dangerously close to
> becoming $100,000 diploma mills.  Nobody really cares what one
> student tells another, and especially no one cares what one
> student thinks about another student's work.

Since he hasn't backed up his claim (that grad students are not
competent to assist in teaching) with any evidence, these last two
paragraphs have no logical leg on which to stand.

>      Perhaps what we will have to do is grade university degrees
> by the year in which they were granted, much as we now grade fine
> wines.

And when did *he* get *his* degree?

