From caf-talk Caf Jun 15 21:02:43 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: proberts@Xenon.Stanford.EDU (Paul Roberts)
Subject: Re: "Censored: Faculty Who Oppose Vivisection", by Joan Dunayer (Z MAGAZINE, APRIL 1993)
Message-ID: <1993Jun16.004926.5501@CSD-NewsHost.Stanford.EDU>
Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 00:49:26 GMT

    ( cross-posted from talk.politics.animals, since the subject
      matter is very much academic freedom )

>   [ Reproduced without permission from Z MAGAZINE, APRIL 1993 - PR ] 
> 
>               Censored: Faculty Who Oppose Vivisection
> 
>                            By Joan Dunayer
> 
>
>"I am morally and ethically opposed to activities that exploit or inflict 
> pain and suffering on nonhuman animals."  So stated veterinarian Nedim 
> Buyukmihci in a 1989 federal suit charging the University of California (UC) 
> with violating his right to free speech. A tenured professor of ophthalmology
> at the UC Davis (UCD) Veterinary School, Buyukmihci for years performed 
> vivisection. Then he rejected the practice and adopted an animal rights 
> philosophy; UCD responded with censorship.
> 
> At a UC Berkeley animal facility in 1983, Buyukmihci saw turtles, with 
> surgical incisions, crowded in shallow filthy water; roosters caged without 
> room to stand upright; and many other animals kept in "appalling" conditions. 
> He informed government officials, as well as animal protection organizations 
> that publicized his findings. Phyllis Dolhinow, chair of Berkeley's Committee 
> for Protection of Animal Subjects, reacted with an indignant letter to 
> Buyukmihci. Dismissing the witnessed filth and crowding as "true of all 
> facilities at times," she accused him of slighting "those of us" who 
> experiment on animals. Dolhinow copied her letter to Vet School dean Edward 
> Rhode and to James Meyer, then UCD chancellor. As revealed by confidential
> memos later subpoenaed, the chancellor's office initiated an investigation of 
> Buyukmihci's animal protection activities, with the stated purpose of 
uncovering grounds for misconduct charges. None resulted.

Soon after, UCD campaigned against a California bill to ban "pound seizure," 
the appropriation of shelter animals by research or teaching institutions.
Buyukmihci testified in favor of the ban. While UCD sought to maintain its 
cheap supply of homeless dogs and cats for dissection and vivisection, 
Buyukmihci stated that the University subjected many shelter animals to 
painful procedures. He urged that dog and cat overpopulation be prevented, 
not exploited. Unable to silence Buyukmihci's public voice, UCD administrators 
proceeded to diminish his voice within the University. When Buyukmihci's 
first term on UCD's Animal Use and Care Committee expired, Rhode sent Meyer 
a confidential memo asking that Buyukmihci be denied reappointment "because 
of his activist and public role in advancing animal rights." Buyukmihci never 
regained membership.

For one semester, Buyukmihci and a more traditional colleague co-taught 
"Ethical and Conceptual Problems in the Use of Animals," a course Buyukmihci 
had conceived. Then Buyukmihci was removed as co-leader - to placate, he 
recalled, "some faculty members who disliked my animal rights views."

In fall 1987, Buyukmihci was preparing to teach Veterinary Ophthalmology 
(VMD 422) for the fourth consecutive year. The course included one eye-surgery
practice session. Each year, Buyukmihci had offered alternatives to practicing 
on healthy dogs who would afterward be killed: any student could practice 
instead on a cadaver or, under Buyukmihci's supervision, on a dog needing the 
surgery. Now the Vet School's Curriculum Committee ruled such alternatives 
unacceptable. Buyukmihci appealed. In December, several weeks before the 
course's scheduled start, he was dismissed as course leader.

By memo, Buyukmihci informed students and offered to approach the new 
instructor on behalf of anyone averse to practicing eye surgery on a healthy 
dog. He also assisted his replacement by providing notes, slides, and test 
questions. Nevertheless, Dean Rhode and Associate Dean George Cardinet, in 
a January 1988 letter to UCD chancellor Theodore Hullar, accused Buyukmihci 
of faculty misconduct and requested "the fullest disciplinary sanctions" - 
dismissal from the University. Buyukmihci, they charged, had failed to abide 
by the Curriculum Committee's ruling, "interfered with die conduct of VMD 
422," attempted to "coerce the judgment or conscience" of VMD 422 students, 
and shown "lack of respect" for his colleagues' opinions. In July, a letter 
from Hullar first notified Buyukmihci of the charges; by this time, VMD 422 
had concluded without incident. Buyukmihci filed suit against UC.

At a 1989 preliminary hearing, the presiding federal judge concluded that 
Buyukmihci had "demonstrated a likelihood of success on the merits of his 
claim that all four charges of faculty misconduct were brought for the 
improper purpose of silencing his speech." With trial imminent, UC in August 
1991 formally accepted liability, agreeing to drop all charges and pay 
Buyukmihci $75,000 in damages, plus his attorneys' fees.

In censoring Buyukmihci, UCD has sought to censor the scientific and moral 
objections to vivisection. Between species, bones and organs differ in 
structure and function; viruses and chemicals harmless to some species are 
deadly to others. Because species differ in anatomy and physiology, 
observations in nonhuman animals are not a valid basis for conclusions about 
humans. To what extent do particular findings in another species apply to 
humans? No one can know without comparing the "animal" data to the 
corresponding human data. But if the human data is available, the animal 
data is superfluous. In lieu of human data, animal data is grossly unreliable. 
Of 197 drugs surveyed by Britain's Centre for Medicines Research, 80 percent
failed human trials after passing animal tests-because in humans the drugs 
were ineffective or harmful. In Monitoring for Drug Safety, physician Richard
Smithells states, "Animal tests can never predict the actions of drugs on 
humans."

Historical evidence counters claims that vivisection conquered such diseases 
as polio. By 1900, autopsies of polio victims had indicated that polio virus 
infects gastrointestinal tissue. For decades after, however, rhesus monkeys 
experimentally exposed to the virus showed infection only in neural tissue. 
Wrongly, researchers concluded they must use neural tissue to grow polio 
virus for vaccines; these proved unacceptably dangerous. Finally, in 1948, 
Enders, Weller, and Robbins confirmed long-disregarded clinical truth by
growing polio virus in human intestinal tissue. Use of non-neural tissue 
yielded a safe vaccine. Albert Sabin, who developed the oral polio vaccine, 
has testified before Congress: "the work on prevention was long delayed by 
an erroneous conception of the nature of the human disease based on misleading 
experimental models of the disease in monkeys."

How effective is contemporary vivisection? According to Dr. Irwin Bross, 
former director of biostatistics at Roswell Park Memorial Institute for 
Cancer Research, animal tests "have never produced a single substantial 
advance either in the prevention or treatment of human cancer." Similarly, 
physician Stephen Kaufman, chair of the Medical Research Modemization 
Committee (MRMC), credits only nonanimal research methods with significant 
progress against AIDS. Epidemiology and clinical studies, he notes, revealed 
the modes of AIDS transmission; using human cell-cultures, researchers 
identified HIV as the AIDS virus and now screen drugs for anti-HIV activity.
Kaufman, in fact, considers vivisection "an overall failure." MRMC physicians 
and scientists hue examined medical research on scores of diseases and 
consistently found that animal experimentation has made no major contribution 
to their prevention or treat ent.

Most vivisection is, in any case, glaringly nonmedical. In routine 
experimentation, researchers have, for example: electrically shocked cats
 - in their eyelids - up to 100 times daily, to see how quickly the cats 
would come to blink at a tone originally paired with each shock; blinded 
hundreds of male sparrows to determine the effect on testicle weight (no 
effect); for days, confined young chimpanzees to isolation chambers, 
observed them scream and hurl themselves against the walls, and concluded, 
"Isolation in infant chimpanzees may be accompanied by marked behavioral 
changes."

Each year in the U.S., at least tens of millions of animals undergo 
vivisection. Throughout their laboratory lives, most are confined to small, 
barren cages. No experimental procedure, however painful or terrifying, 
is prohibited. No pain-killers are required. (The Animal Welfare Act 
regulates only housing and transport of some mammals.) And so, animals are 
regularly burned, starved, irradiated, and otherwise caused intense suffering.

Believing that all animals should be free from human exploitation and abuse, 
animal rights advocates necessarily oppose vivisection, arguing as follows.
Useful or not, vivisection rests on victimization (as did human slavery, 
which was also defended with claims of usefulness). Like race or sex, species 
simply provides an excuse for exploitation. U other animals cannot read, 
solve equations, or create art, neither can many humans. Other animals can 
experience deprivation and pain. If, then, it is wrong to vivisect humans 
of whatever intellectual capacity-because they can suffer, it is wrong to 
vivisect other animals for precisely the same reason. Because vivisection 
imprisons, torments, and destroys innocent beings, it epitomizes injustice 
- and should be abolished.

Why would the academic establishment suppress the reasons for opposing 
vivisection? To large extent, vivisection resides within academia. Many 
who teach psychology, biology, or medicine have built their careers on 
the practice. Institutions, too, have a vested interest in preserving 
vivisection, which brings universities hefty funding. According to UCD's 
Office of Research, the year Buyukmihci filed suit (1989) UCD received 
$76 million in direct grants for research involving nonhuman animals.
A direct grant covers a project's estimated costs. Along with each federal
direct grant, however, an institution receives an additional sum for 
research overhead (indirect costs such as building maintenance); this sum 
is a set percentage of the direct grant - 41 percent at UCD in 1989. Usually
more expensive than human clinical research projects, animal research 
projects generally receive larger federal grants, which bring larger 
overhead allotments. Vivisection is a multi-billion-dollar industry, 
and academia shares in the profits.

Like UCD, Stanford University has censored animal rights. In fall 1988, 
Stanford Workshops on Political and Social Issues (SWOPSI) included an 
animal rights course taught by Use Giraud, a retired Stanford University
librarian, and Kim Sturla, then director of a humane society. Ten
undergraduate seminars explored the philosophical and political frameworks 
underlying the use of animals as objects of sport and entertainment,
consumer products, and experimental tools. Some guest speakers and assigned 
readings supported the instructors' animal rights viewpoint; others 
represented the opposition.

At one session, a Stanford Medical School professor argued in favor of 
vivisection; a California State University philosophy professor argued 
against. Stanford biology professor Robert Sapolsky, a vivisector, attended. 
Within days, Stanford president Donald Kennedy - formerly a vivisector 
and biology department chair-spoke to SWOPSI director Margo Horn. In a
letter to Kennedy, she addressed his "concern about the SWOPSI course on 
animal rights" and "the Stanford biology community's sensitivity over this 
issue." Defending the course as academically worthy, Horn detailed its
adherence to rigorous standards of "fair representation."

Nevertheless, Thomas Wasow, dean of Undergraduate Studies, soon received 
an unsigned letter that opened "We, the unanimous faculty of the Department
of Biological Sciences, write to protest the inclusion of SWOPSI Course 166,
'Animal Rights - or Wrongs?', in this year's course offerings." The letter 
objected to "who is teaching it," labeling Giraud and Sturla "animal rights 
militants." While admitting that some biology faculty had a "political agenda," mix.com (Paul Roberts) writes:
the letter declared the course unacceptable because the instructors had
failed to "respect for the motives of the opposition" and had previously 
questioned these motives "in public forums." The letter requested 
reevaluation of "the appropriateness of giving such a course in the future, 
either by these particular instructors, or by their associates." The typed 
names of all biology faculty appeared at the bottom, in alphabetical order.

The letter was copied to the associate dean of Humanities, the dean of 
Humanities and Sciences, Horn, and Kennedy; yet, Giraud and Sturla first 
learned of its existence months later. Realizing they might be prevented
from teaching again at Stanford, Giraud and Sturla consulted an attorney, 
Paul McCloskey. On March 6, McCloskey wrote to all biology faculty asking 
whether they had authorized the use of their names on the letter. Three 
faculty members promptly reported they had not.

Hastily the biology department called a meeting. According to a confidential 
source who attended, Sapolsky admitted having written the letter. Those 
present, however, were enjoined to maintain secrecy. When asked, for this 
article, whether he wrote the letter, Sapolsky answered, "I'd like not to 
comment on that," then added, "because of the possible legal ramifications."
Quickly he veered into a general denunciation of animal rights activists.
For years, Sapolsky has subjected rats to isolation, cold, hour-long shaking,
prolonged immobilization, induced seizures, and other "stresses" - noting 
interactions between the resulting brain damage and rats' adrenal hormones.

A March 9 departmental memo informed biology faculty that Horn had agreed 
not to offer the course again, so "Dean Wasow considered the matter closed."
Yet, the next week, the University's legal office assured McCloskey: 
"Dean Wasow has informed us that any proposal by Ms. Giraud and/or Ms. Sturla 
to teach a SWOPSI course would be evaluated in the usual way." No course on 
animal rights has been offered since.

"We were singled out for censorship," says Giraud, "because animal rights 
represents a tremendous economic threat to Stanford University and to all 
other institutions that profit from animal exploitation." Stanford does 
indeed profit from vivisection. In 1988-89, the year the animal rights 
course was effectively banned, Stanford received approximately $83 million
for animal experimentation from the National Institute of Health (NIH) 
alone: $48 million in direct grants and $35 million for overhead. Other 
government agencies and private sources provided additional millions.

A censorship case strikingly similar to Giraud and Sturla's recently 
occurred at the University of Pennsylvania. During the summers of 1988 
and '89, Penn's Discovery Program offered an animal-welfare course
taught by Zoe Weil, an experienced naturalist and humane educator. For a 
week, students probed institutionalized animal abuse, such as that within 
the fur and meat industries. As desired by Discovery Program director 
Susan Duggan, the course omitted any discussion of animal experimentation, 
which Duggan considers "one of the most sensitive issues at the University."

Even so, the course offended one parent, a Wistar Institute animal-research 
technician married to a Wistar vivisection. She complained to Michael 
Sidelsky, manager of Wistar's animal facility, and he alerted Penn 
colleagues. In October, with the course scheduled to be offered again 
in winter 1989, Duggan and Weil met with Sidelsky and Penn Veterinary 
School professor Adrian Morrison. Sidelsky and Morrison examined the photos 
Weil had shown her students, including graphic photos of animal suffering. 
Although the issue of vivisection had already been barred from the
course, Weil says, Morrison and Sidelsky now further censored the course 
content. No literature or photographs on any topic, they insisted, from 
any animal protection organization opposed to vivisection. Weil and Duggan 
acquiesced.

Morrison defended his action, Weil recalls, by claiming that she was 
"biased" whereas he was "objective." For decades, Morrison has vivisected 
cats to observe the effects on their sleep patterns  - crushing their
spinal cords and electrically burning portions of their brains. Today, he 
directs the Office of Animal Research Issues at the Alcohol, Drug Abuse 
and Mental Health Administration; at taxpayers' expense, he champions 
vivisection.

In a November letter to Duggan, Morrison expressed renewed opposition to 
the course. Duggan wrote Weil, "I am bowing to the pressures," which 
included a recent phone call from Vet School dean Edwin Andrews. Duggan 
canceled the course.

At Weil's request, Penn's Committee on Academic Freedom and Responsibility 
investigated. They ruled: "The circumstances that led to the cancellation 
of the course constituted a clear infringement of academic freedom." 
Duggan, they found, had been subjected to "undue pressure, verging upon 
harassment." The course, however, was not rescheduled.

"Although I was previously aware of universities' incentive to exploit 
nonhuman animals," Weil says, "I was shocked to find that those in positions 
of power could so easily suppress academic freedom."  The year that Weil's 
course was quashed (1989-90), Penn's incentive included approximately 
$67 million for animal experimentation from the NIH alone, $22 million of
this for overbead.

Formerly a Penn law professor, Gary Francione remembers Penn as a 
"thoroughly corrupt institution" that "does not respect free speech." 
Penn administrators, he says, repeatedly threatened him with career 
penalties for publicizing animal abuse within the University. Now director 
of the Rutgers Animal Rights Law Clinic, Francione considers Penn far from 
unique. Over the past few years, he reports, some two dozen college and 
university faculty members have consulted him after experiencing threatened 
or actual professional penalty for expressing an animal protection viewpoint. 
"Nationwide," Francione has concluded, "there is a rapidly growing trend 
within academia to penalize those who advocate animal rights."

Lacking political power, nonhuman animals are most vulnerable to 
exploitation. Academia has taken full advantage of that vulnerability. 
Other animals cannot speak for themselves; when their advocates are 
silenced, the censorship is doubly tragic. 

