Someone opined that a system administrator could not forbid the
possession of cracking software.  It was said that "you can't
say that PD code is illegal."

There's a BIG difference between "illegal" and "not permitted
here."  If I say, "Cracking software is not allowed on this
system," I can't (and won't) stop you from building said soft-
ware on another system.  For all I care, you can build it on
every system to which you have access *except* mine.

In this example, I'm not claiming that it's illegal; I'm merely
setting a restriction on your use of this particular resource.
We could say the same thing about GIFs; I don't care if you fill
50 PC floppies with GIFs, but I can certainly refuse to give you
extra disk space to store them on my systems.

[ This is a completely hypothetical situation; I'll give Crack
[ and cbw to any user who requests it.  I'll even provide some
[ 'sanitized' /etc/passwd files, so you can see how Crack works
[ without using live password files.  I did this very thing for
[ our course in Computer and Information Security last summer.
[ (No, I won't send this stuff to remote users; I can't know 
[ the policy of every site, and I won't run the risk of com-
[ pounding your potential problems.)

In my opinion, it's completely reasonable to forbid certain
types of software *as long as* the ban applies equally to all
users.  Of course, I don't actively check for this stuff; I
won't say a word unless something *really* odd turns up in
my logfiles.  I don't monitor users.

--Wes

