Crack plus a copy of a real local passwd file adds up pretty nicely,
particularly if the dates show that it's been around for a while, and
you already have evidence that *someone* has been breaking in
recently.  As I said before, the memos posted do not give enough
details to really judge this incident.  If I noticed Crack (and, yes,
I do have a little script that scans all file systems for
"interesting" packages like Crack and COPS, based on included files
that are unlikely to be renamed), and it had obviously just been
downloaded, the user would get a nice message discussing the hazards
of attempting to use the software "against" our system.

>Is there ever a point where circumstantial evidence alone is good
>enough to file charges?

I would say no, but it's definitely enough to start an informal
investigation.  To ward off protest rallies, I'll clarify that
"informal investigation" does not mean "read all of user's files and
mail, spy on terminal sessions, and tell operators to watch out for
the person".  Most likely, it means "make a cursory scan of user's
directory, looking for unusual datafiles, cracking software, 'hidden'
directories (I love these; why don't they just put a big sign up
saying NAUGHTY BITS HERE), etc, and check the login logs for unusual
usage patterns (coming in from out-of-state and on-campus in the same
day, for instance)".  Often the guilty party isn't the real user, so
pressing charges would be premature.

>I mean really, crack is available at bunches of anonymous ftp sites.
