>him send a retraction and I fully admit my guilt, but I have a problem
>with the professors reading e-mail. I'd like to send him any info
>I get on it so he will atleast think of the unethical means he uses
>to gather his evidence.

This raises a very interesting sociological issue. I admit to being kinda   
old, compared to many (most?) participants here, so I may be out of touch. I 
welcome attempts to enlighten me.
                                                        
When I went to college, in the early 1950's the accepted social doctrine was 
that universities were "in loco parentis." That is--they had certain
obligations to act as surrogate parents to their undergraduates in some
matters. Thus they were seen to have an ethical teaching responsibility,
which meant that the rigors of full adult "due process" were seen not to
apply, but rather a separate series of principles, call it "academic
institution due process" were used. 
                         
There's another societal institution with its own set of practices--the   
military, which even in peacetime uses the "Uniform Code of Military
Justice", quite different in many ways from ordinary civil law. The reasons
are distinct from those in the academic case, of course.

If the 'in loco parentis' doctrine is still applied, then appeal to wider
civil liberties laws may be unsuccessful--one needs instead to appeal to the
institution's own code of due process.

David

