From: otto@garnet.acns.fsu.edu (John G. Otto)
Subject: comments on proposed policy
Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 21:03:02 GMT
Message-ID: <otto-160693170807@hotline.cc.fsu.edu>

Comments on proposed "Guidelines for Use of Compus Computer & Network
Resources" at the Florida STATE University.

Paragraph 3 "Those who avail themselves of the campus and network
   computing resources, which includes electronic communications
   (i.e. "E-mail"), are required to use the technology in a manner
   consistent with Chapter 815, Florida Statutes Computer Crimes Act
   and Title 18, United States Code, Electronic Communications Privacy
   Act of 1986.  Unauthorized or fraudulent use of the University's
   computing resources is a serious violation and may result in felony
   prosecution and punishment as provided for in Florida Statutes,
   Chapter 775, Florida Criminal Code.  Offenses against intellectual
   property such as duplicating software which is protected by copyright
   or trade secret law is also a violation of the law."

     This neglects to recognize article 1 section 23 of the Florida
     constitution which is superior to the state statutes mentioned.
     Also improperly neglected are the Privacy Act of 1974 (PA1974) and
     the Family Educational Right to Privacy Act of 1974 (FERPA1974),
     Florida Statutes 228.093, and article 4 section 2, article 6,
     amendments 1, 4, 5, 9, & 14 of the US constitution all of which bear
     on the use of "campus and network computing resources, which
     includes electronic communications" especially with respect to
     ownership of intellectual property and privacy.

Paragraph 9, responsibility number 2:  "Do not intentionally seek
   information about, browse, obtian copies of, or modify files, passwords,
   or tapes belonging to any other person, whether at FSU or elsewhere,
   unless specifically authorized to do so by that individual.  (Note: if
   an individual has explicitly and intentionally established a public
   server, or clearly designated a set of files as being for shared public
   use, others may assume authorization.)"

     Owners of files have the ability to restrict access or to make them
     publicly accessible.  If files are publicly accessible and
unencrypted,
     it is not appropriate either to assume that they are meant to be
private
     or that they are meant to be public, i.e. accessing such files without
     further information should be considered impolite but not criminal.
     Customers, developers and administrators should be fully informed
     concerning the means by which they can control access.

Paragraph 13, responsibility number 6:  "Be sensitive to the public nature
   of shared facilities.  University computing resources do not provide for
   sending or receiving private or confidential electronic communications.
   All messages shall be deemed to be readily accessible to the general
   public.  Thus, do not use this system for any communication for which
the
   sender intends only the sender and the intended recipient(s) to read.
   Take care when using general access computers not to display images,
   sounds or messages which could create an atmosphere of discomfort or
   harassment [sic] for others.  Refrain from transmitting to others in any
   location inappropriate images, sounds or messages which might reasonably
   be considered harassing.

     The first sentence somewhat contradicts paragraphs 6 and 9.
     Sentences 2, 3 & 4 contradict paragraphs 3, 6, & 9, specifically
     ECPA1986 (see paragraph 3 for other applicable law), which recognizes
     e-mail to be private.  The rest contradicts the first amendment of
     the US constitution, is subjective and arbitrary, and may require
     psychic powers for one to avoid violating it.

     I have heard some make an analogy between e-mail and paper snail, that
     they should be treated the same as regards privacy.  The conclusion to
     be made therefrom is that both e-mail and snail should be considered
     private.  A post received 1993-04-19 from a law professor concerning
     the 1978 changes to the US copyright laws suggests that not only is it
     private but protected as "an unpublished work" from copying by the
     recipient without permission of the sender.  The sole exceptions are
     statements of public policy, records of government meetings and
similar
     public documents.
Enough for now...jgo

