You should be aware of how different many colleges are from the one you
seem to have come from.  When you went to college a legal adult was
someone over 21.  'In loco parentis' can be seen as a valid concept when
most students are not even legal adults, but you are a legal adult at 18 now.

At many universities there are additional circumstances that make the
concept of 'in loco parentis' inappropriate.  If my memory serves me right
at the University of Houston the average age of a student in math 1431
(calc I) is 26 years old.  Well past the age of legal adulthood even in the
50's.   In evening classes the average age goes up even further.  It is not
at all unusual to have someone miss a class because their 13 year old child
had to be taken to the doctor.  Are these people to be treated as children?

Issues like this make 'in loco parentis' inappropriate for modern
educational institutions.
