The key will probably be in your campus computing policies, and what they say 
about the privacy of your electronic mail.  If they establish that faculty 
members have the access described by your professor, you may not have any
recourse.  But I have my doubts, and another response has suggested you 
file a complaint with the proper academic officials.  My sense is that most
academic senates would oppose this kind of monitoring.

The principle is similar to those discussed in the recent Epson case and 
those like it, where a company email system allowed supervisors access to
email.  Most people seem to oppose it in principle, there are good arugments 
supporting it from a business perspective, and the notice you were, or were
not given, is critical.

And yes, there are laws agains *unauthorized* people snooping in your email.
The question is, who is and is not authorized?

