In article <1vn8ia$kjc@usenet.INS.CWRU.Edu> al752@cleveland.Freenet.Edu (Wes Morgan) writes:

> There's a BIG difference between "illegal" and "not permitted
> here."  If I say, "Cracking software is not allowed on this
> system," I can't (and won't) stop you from building said soft-
> ware on another system.  For all I care, you can build it on
> every system to which you have access *except* mine.

> In my opinion, it's completely reasonable to forbid certain
> types of software *as long as* the ban applies equally to all
> users.  Of course, I don't actively check for this stuff; I
> won't say a word unless something *really* odd turns up in
> my logfiles.  I don't monitor users.

Amen!

Forbidding the use of cracking software is perfectly reasonable.
Just because it is publicly available and probably legal does not mean
that it is a valid use of resources. It is not allowed here. Most
academic systems are not rent-per-hour public systems; they are funded
with specific uses in mind, such as course and research support, and
other uses should be at the discretion of the university. As long as
the rules are fair, clearly spelled out to the users, and enforced in
an even and just manner, it is reasonable to have rules.

The "Crack" software is a very dangerous piece of work, with few
legitimate uses. Our policy is that if someone DOES have a legitimate
use for this software (doing a paper on security, say, or running a
home unix system) they must get our permission before using this 
software. This software is the closest thing to a set of burglary
tools that there is online, and our users could threaten not only our
own system but any other system on the Net with it. It would be quite
anti-social of this to allow our system to be a base for attempts on
other systems. 
  Having said that there might be some legitimate reasons for running
Crack, every use I've seen has been hostile.
  Enforcement of the rules is another thing. There are several factors
to take into account: Does the user have binaries? Does the user have
our password file? Other site's passwd files? Does the user have lists
of cracked passwd's? Is there evidence that the user has *used* other
user's passwords?
   Breaking into another user's account, that is, both obtaining and
USING another user's password, is a clear violation of our
University's Academic policy and we would certainly press charges
(penalties which range from failing a course through suspension,
depending on the circumstances.) The other situations are more
ambiguous and call for lesser punishment, IMHO. 

By the way, we have the opposite problem here; our university has not
taken action against a malicious cracker that we caught. Our
department can ban this user from our departmental computers, but we
can't make the administration take action.
  Our definition of malicious: using Crack to break into another
user's account and sending obscene e-mail to that student's professor
in that student's name. Stupid, stupid, stupid.

Betsy

