Tim Smith (tzs@stein2.u.washington.edu) wrote:
: elee9sf@menudo.menudo.UH.EDU (Karl Barrus) writes:
: >>   I'm not an attorney, but my reading tells me that "intent" is one of the
: >>   elements of a crime, and can be inferred by actions.
: >
: >To paraphrase you: unless you have significant mindreading abilities,
: >you cannot make this statement.
: 
: Huh?  I go into a bank.  I hand the teller a note that says "Give me $50,000
: or I'll blow your head off."  I show a gun.  Are you saying that unless the
: jury has significant mind reading abilities, I can't be properly convicted
: of attempted bank robbery?  (Yes, at the trial I'm going to say that I only
: intended it as a joke, and was not going to actually take the money).
: 
: Intent usually is required for crime, and since criminals often don't
: want to admit that they had intent, it is usually inferred by actions.
: There's no need for mindreading, just for a weighing of probabilities
: to get beyond a reasonable doubt.

I think we need to get back to the original context of this discussion.

If i copy a computer etc/passwd file and then break into that computer
you can infer that i copied the passwd file with the intent to use it
to break into the computer.  Similarly, if i purchase a gun, pencil
and paper and then use them to rob a bank, as described above, you can
infer intent in the purchases.

If, on the other hand, i only copy the passwd file, or purchase a gun,
pencil and paper, but never actually break into the computer or rob
the bank you cannot infer my intent.  You could only know my intent in
these cases by reading my mind.  I haven't seen any statement that the
passwd file copier in this case made any later effort to actually
break into the computer.  So it would require mind reading to know
that he has such an intention.

While i can understand that intent may be a factor in the prosecution
and sentencing after a crime has been committed (since some crimes are
clearly unintentional), i will be (even more) worried if our
government has degenerated to the point where intent by itself is a
crime.

