The worst allegations I've seen here were on the documents that were
supposedly signed by all parties, ie the summaries of the previous
counseling sessions.  Point after point demonstrated a complete indifference
to the responsibilities and accountabilties of anyone outside the very
specific interactions with KC.  It read very much like a case of "what can
be gotten away with?  Ok, do at least that."

Over and over the theme has recurred here that a college environment should
promote learning.  I happen to agree.  I don't agree, however, that this
should be directly solely to technical issues.  There are people in this
world and learning to work with them is at least as important as learning
how a MUD works or what Crack can do.  The less intelligent and/or smart
the people you meet are, the more important - not less - it becomes to
learn how to function with them instead of against them.  You will NOT be
successful if you base it entirely on the letter of the law, or if you
are you'll be a very rare case.  Being exactly right is very little help
when you've developed the persona and reputation of a person nearly no one
cares to work with.

I could put a big disclaimer here that I don't know the details of this
case, but it appears nobody knows all the details.  I'm no different.  But
there are definate impressions being given about all the participants,
and many of those impressions have been presented by those claiming a
fair amount of knowledge (licit or otherwise).  I don't think, though,
that all the impressions imparted were what these proponents intended.

Wes has presented a lot of reasoned responses, and I think Alan had some
things to say that very much deserve listening too.  Neither have said
that KC and friends should be burned at that stake or anything like that,
but they have presented impressions that were fostered by a lot of what
