the government for a redress of grievances.
 
The intent of the inclusion of freedom of the press was that watching and 
reporting about the government and its activities would keep the people
alert to the abuse and potential abuse of power. However to be considered
part of the press in New York City, you have to have a "Press Pass". To get
one of these, you have to apply to the New York City Police Department, the
guards of the gates of authority themselves. 

Now lest anyone start with the "you can sue them for abuse of power argument", 
you have to prove "intent" for that. They don't actually deny anyone outright
unless they have a darned good reason. You cannot however, sue them for being 
incompetent, and a lot of stuff conveniently gets lost or temporarily
misplaced.

Mr. Sternlight argues in the first quoted article that his attitude towards
authority cannot be judged because we aren't mindreaders. Yet in the very next
post he states that the punishment of the crime was justified because the 
"intent" was obvious. This is the kind of double edged argument that people 
in authority use to justify the careful trimming away at the edges of liberty 
in the name of "we know best - so trust us". We can't judge them because we can
not possibly know what they are thinking, but they can prosecute us because
it is obvious what we "would have done" had they not been alert enough to 
stop us.

If I have to make a choice, I think I come down on the side of Ben Franklin
on this matter when he said:

"They that can give up essential liberty to obtain a little temporary
           safety deserve neither liberty nor safety."
