>On systems I am on, any user can view a userlist.  But they don't have the
>AUTHORITY to modify it or take the list to other systems unless permission
>is given.  They can obtain the data in the userlist in one or many of the
>authorized methods (finger, who, etc.) that ROOT has authorized.  
>
>But, if I am misinterpreting your former access then you have NO AUTHORITY to
>do what you want the files owned by ROOT.  You can access the data in those
>files in ways that ROOT permits but not more.

I don't understand what you think you're saying.  I imagine that /etc/passwd
at uh looks a lot like /etc/passwd here:

-rw-r--r-- root        465 Jun 17 14:02 /etc/passwd

So *anyone* on the system can READ (and therefore print, copy, make cryptograms
out of, write out 100,000 times on little squares of toilet paper and mail to
Botswana) /etc/passwd, but only root can actually WRITE (and therefore change)
it.  root permits all READ operations.  Printing out a file does not constitute
authority to change it, to add users, or anything of the sort.  Unless you
merely expressed yourself extremely poorly, you have no clue whatsoever what
you're talking about.  However, to give you the benefit of the doubt, in case
you _are_ aware that being able to dump /etc/passwd does not imply modification
privileges and merely stated it incomprehensibly:

It is clearly impractical for a sysadmin to give "authorization" for _each_and_
every_ allowable use of /etc/passwd.  Sure, "finger" is one.  But as was
pointed out, "ls" is another--something _I_, and I suspect many sysadmins,
didn't know.  There are probably hundreds of common utilities that access
/etc/passwd, which, unless you'd written them or pored over the source code,
you wouldn't _a_priori_ know.  I seriously doubt that many sysadmins are that
wizardly, and there's no reason to expect them to be.  Separating access
from authority would only work in a rigidly controlled setting, in which 
the only software allowed would be that approved by the administrators.  This
clearly is impractical in an educational environment, although it might work
in military sites and some anal-retentive companies.  Remember that it would
therefore require special permission to use the C compiler.  Let alone, gasp,
a debugger.

Access and authorization _are_ basically synonymous in Unix systems.  I can,
offhand, think of at least _one_ good and non-sinister, quite legal reason,
to grab /etc/passwd: it's the easiest and quickest way to provide a one-to-
one map between userids and real names.  Which, if you're going to another
system, is a very good thing to have so you can keep in touch with your
friends.  Apparently 'finger @@' provides the same functionality.  This is
not generic across Unices; I suspect it to be a BSD 4.2 perversion.

If /etc/passwd had been chmod'ed 600, then maybe you would have some sort of
point.  I'd be most amused to see a usable Unix system with a -rw-------
/etc/passwd.

