> their services, and the results are probably not as good.

This assumes that profs are paid solely to give lectures.  Teaching
involves more than the actual act of standing in front of a blackboard.
One important aspect of running a course is choosing the material to be
taught.  This requires intimate and up-to-date expertise in the field,
especially in fast-moving areas like computer science and genetics, so
that the prof can make the course relevant and useful.  While I can't
speak for Yale, I know that at both McGill and MIT, course content is
almost always determined by the prof, who acts as an administrator for
the course.  In rapidly-changing areas, even the undergraduate courses
are updated regularly.  The graduate student may have less background in
the field to know what are the most important areas of his field, but he
can competently explain the basics to the class once the prof tells him
what areas to cover.

In my earlier post, I compared the university environment to other types
of hierarchical structures.  My specific example was a surgical team, where
the master brain surgeon leaves the sewing up to a junior surgeon.  Yes, the
end result may be marginally inferior, but it would be a poor use of the
head surgeon's time to do otherwise.

This type of structure is common in the real world.  Ideally, the person
at the top of the heap is an expert in the area.  He isn't paid to sit
there and do all the work himself.  He is paid to manage a team, to guide
members of the team, give expert advice, and coordinate things so that
workers are optimally allocated.  It's the same in academia.

As for comparing basic teaching ability, I'd say it's a toss-up.  The
worst class I ever had was a recitation taught by a junior professor (one
student wrote on his evaluation: "If I were Prof. ______'s father, I'd ask
for my sperm back!"), while I've had some very good grad student instructors.
It probably comes down to the usual enthusiasm-vs-experience comparison.
Older profs will hopefully have honed their teaching skills, but a grad
student who is "barely a step or two ahead of the students" may be better
able to identify with a student's struggle to grasp a concept, since
the grad student went through the same learning process only recently.

